statement:
On every HAL tau-bench airline cell (model, scaffold) that holds two archived runs, pair the 41 tasks across the two runs and split each disagreement in stored reward into a trigger flip (graded in one run and not the other, using the card's grader-action alignment to mark graded episodes) or an outcome flip (both graded, rewards differ); then re-grade every ungraded episode offline through the pinned tau-bench commit, as the paper does, and recompute the disagreement with every episode graded. Readouts per cell: the published disagreement $D_{\mathrm{pub}} =  rac{1}{n}\sum_i \mathbb{1}[r_{i1} 
eq r_{i2}]$ (task $i$, runs 1 and 2, stored reward $r_{ir} \in \{0,1\}$, $n = 41$), the trigger share $	au = |\{i : G_{i1} 
eq G_{i2},\ r_{i1} 
eq r_{i2}\}| \,/\, |\{i : r_{i1} 
eq r_{i2}\}|$ with $G_{ir} \in \{0,1\}$ the grading indicator, the stability of the ungraded set $J = |U_1 \cap U_2| / |U_1 \cup U_2|$ with $U_r = \{i : G_{ir} = 0\}$ (Jaccard overlap, 1 when the same tasks go ungraded in both runs), and the all-graded disagreement $D_{\mathrm{all}}$ computed from $\hat r_{ir} = G_{ir} r_{ir} + (1 - G_{ir}) 	ilde r_{ir}$, $	ilde r_{ir}$ the offline re-grade. Zero agent runs if at least three Generalist cells hold two runs; otherwise one new Generalist run per model for three models under HAL's released scaffold supplies the second run.
